b. acts with or without the victim's consent which include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, exploitation of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully removing or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or exploiting the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain benefits both material and immaterial; and/orc. all forms of exploitation of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, including all activities of prostitution or sexual abuse.

4.1.text:
Article 21

1. To prevent violence against women and children, the government conducts empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government undertakes efforts as follows:a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; andg. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from violence.

Article 22

1. Prevention of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 21, is implemented by regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:a. Social;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual; andg. public peace and order.
2. Prevention of violence by regional apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 3

1. The purpose of implementing protection for women and child victims of violence is to:a. prevent violence against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children; d. provide services to women and child victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses;
e. facilitate and mediate domestic disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household; andf. Empowerment of women and child victims of violence.
2. The purpose of implementing protection for women and child victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), includes aspects of:
a. prevention;
b. service and assistance;
c. reintegration and rehabilitation; andd. empowerment.

Article 13

1. The Regional Government implements efforts to protect women and children victims of violence through:a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children victims of violence adopted by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. establishment and implementation of the functions of P2TP2A;
d. ensuring the implementation of ease of service to victims;
e. seeking effectiveness and efficiency for the victim recovery process; andf. seeking the creation of cooperation and coordination in victim recovery efforts.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the regional government establishes action programs and activities for the protection of women and children in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as a basis for regional apparatus in implementing protection for women and children victims of violence.
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), is further regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 14

1. The obligations and responsibilities of society as referred to in Article 12 letter b, are implemented in the form of community participation. (2) Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), include:a. forming family partners at the sub-district level;
b. forming women and child protection units within community organizations;
c. conducting independent socialization of women's and children's rights; d. providing first aid to victims; ande. reporting to the competent authority if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.
4. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), are implemented responsibly.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The implementation of protection for women and child victims of violence is carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of victims of violence; b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. the best interests of victims;
e. empowerment of women and child victims of violence.

CHAPTER III

FORMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 4

Forms of violence against women and children may include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; ande. exploitation.

CHAPTER VII

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 28

1. The Regional Government conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence.
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:  a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring; ande. evaluation (3) Supervision as referred to in paragraph (1), is conducted with the principles of:a. professional;
b. transparent; andc. accountable.

Article 25

1. Forms of services provided to women and children victims of violence organized by UPT, include:a. Medical services in the form of treatment and recovery of physical condition carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. Medicolegal services constitute a form of medical service for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. Legal aid services to assist victims of violence in undergoing the judicial process;
d. Spiritual services;
e. First psychosocial assistance to victims conducted to identify the occurrence of violence and trauma experienced by victims; f. Assistance to victims of violence carried out by competent persons or institutions that have expertise to conduct counseling, therapy and advocacy for the strengthening and recovery of victims of violence and have cooperated with UPT; andg. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and provision of economic access so that victims can be independent.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), are implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:  a. social;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; andd. mental and spiritual.
3. In carrying out duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government cooperates with government agencies, provincial government, other district/city governments, the community, families and parents.
4. Further provisions regarding procedures for services, and handling of women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3), are regulated in a Mayor Regulation.

CHAPTER V

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 12

Obligations and responsibilities in the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence are the joint responsibility of:  a. regional government;
b. society;
c. family; andd. parents.

Article 7

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 4 letter c includes:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/ord. forced sexual intercourse with another person for commercial purposes and or certain purposes.

Article 11

Child victims of violence, in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 10, also receive special rights, as follows:  a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; ande. the right to obtain freedom.

